{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018Sq1AuKHE1i9SLNmxHz13u","type":"tool_result","content":"139:    - \"set-based-selector\"\n269:- **Select** a set of objects by label using both equality-based and set-based syntax, and explain why an annotation cannot be selected on.\n804:**Set-based** selectors use `in`, `notin`, and `exists` (plus its negation). For example, `environment in (production, qa)`, `tier notin (frontend, backend)`, `partition` (meaning: has the key at all), and `!partitio\n828:Older Kubernetes APIs took selectors as flat strings. Newer resources (**Job, Deployment, ReplicaSet, and DaemonSet**) support set-based requirements through two structured fields, `matchLabels` and `matchExpressions\n886:**1.** 🔵 Write the equality-based form and the set-based form of the same requirement: *objects whose `environment` label is `production`.*\n910:**1.** Equality-based: `environment = production` (or `environment == production`). Set-based: `environment in (production)` [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]. They express the same requirement; set-base\n919:`matchLabels` is a map of `{key, value}` pairs equivalent to `matchExpressions` with operator `In` [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]. If you wrote operator `Equals`, that is not one of the set-based oper\n1153:**Q17.** 🔵 Which of these is a valid **set-based** selector?","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f9b02ac-049e-468e-9571-70af8edf8dd5","uuid":"4adc053d-bdef-4177-96a6-69bfdecf77c5","timestamp":"2026-08-24T19:46:21.171Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"139:    - \"set-based-selector\"\n269:- **Select** a set of objects by label using both equality-based and set-based syntax, and explain why an annotation cannot be selected on.\n804:**Set-based** selectors use `in`, `notin`, and `exists` (plus its negation). For example, `environment in (production, qa)`, `tier notin (frontend, backend)`, `partition` (meaning: has the key at all), and `!partitio\n828:Older Kubernetes APIs took selectors as flat strings. Newer resources (**Job, Deployment, ReplicaSet, and DaemonSet**) support set-based requirements through two structured fields, `matchLabels` and `matchExpressions\n886:**1.** 🔵 Write the equality-based form and the set-based form of the same requirement: *objects whose `environment` label is `production`.*\n910:**1.** Equality-based: `environment = production` (or `environment == production`). Set-based: `environment in (production)` [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]. They express the same requirement; set-base\n919:`matchLabels` is a map of `{key, value}` pairs equivalent to `matchExpressions` with operator `In` [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]. If you wrote operator `Equals`, that is not one of the set-based oper\n1153:**Q17.** 🔵 Which of these is a valid **set-based** selector?","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
