{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T7YzocEbscaQ7QapL1W8wC","type":"tool_result","content":"17:**A decay problem I flagged and fixed:** Ch 8's version-skew block is the densest pure-recall material in the book, taught at the 40% mark and otherwise never revisited before exam day. The schedule deliberately places version skew in Ch 13 (as a troubleshooting cause) and release cadence in Ch 17 (where the three-supported-minors rule and the ~3/year cadence explain each other). Ch 7 (scheduling) and Ch 14 (Helm) are similarly thin and get named anchors."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f9b02ac-049e-468e-9571-70af8edf8dd5","uuid":"38b7667c-9649-4005-ac98-699ec0aabab1","timestamp":"2026-08-24T19:49:25.634Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"17:**A decay problem I flagged and fixed:** Ch 8's version-skew block is the densest pure-recall material in the book, taught at the 40% mark and otherwise never revisited before exam day. The schedule deliberately places version skew in Ch 13 (as a troubleshooting cause) and release cadence in Ch 17 (where the three-supported-minors rule and the ~3/year cadence explain each other). Ch 7 (scheduling) and Ch 14 (Helm) are similarly thin and get named anchors.","numLines":1,"totalLines":1}}
