{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018HpUXYV4Mdw9zWMg4MXy65","type":"tool_result","content":"16-\n17:**A decay problem I flagged and fixed:** Ch 8's version-skew block is the densest pure-recall material in the book, taught at the 40% mark and otherwise never revisited before exam day. The schedule deliberately places version skew in Ch 13 (as a troubleshooting cause) and release cadence in Ch 17 (where the three-supported-minors rule and the ~3/year cadence explain each other). Ch 7 (scheduling) and Ch 14 (Helm) are similarly thin and get named anchors.\n18-"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f9b02ac-049e-468e-9571-70af8edf8dd5","uuid":"9d3d891a-a73a-4677-84ed-2efb0b1f626d","timestamp":"2026-08-24T19:49:32.005Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"16-\n17:**A decay problem I flagged and fixed:** Ch 8's version-skew block is the densest pure-recall material in the book, taught at the 40% mark and otherwise never revisited before exam day. The schedule deliberately places version skew in Ch 13 (as a troubleshooting cause) and release cadence in Ch 17 (where the three-supported-minors rule and the ~3/year cadence explain each other). Ch 7 (scheduling) and Ch 14 (Helm) are similarly thin and get named anchors.\n18-","numLines":3,"totalLines":3}}
